# Untitled

## Planar Heterojunction Perovskite Solar Cells Incorporating Metal–Organic Framework Nanocrystals
Hybrid organic-lead halide perovskite solar cells have attracted great attention since Miyasaka and co-workers reported the first perovskite sensitized solar cell in 2009.[1] In the past few years, perovskites have shown unexpectable and rapid progress in the field of solar cells due to the high power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) and relatively low cost. At first, perovskite was served as a sensitizer in an electrolyte-based dye-sensitized solar cell, but this kind of solar cells exhibited low PCEs due to the limited solubility of perovskite in polar solvents.[2] A report on a solid-state perovskite solar cell based on mesoporous structured has achieved a PCE exceeding \(10\%\).[3] It was found that after absorbing light in perovskite, photo-excited electrons would be collected by the underlying electrode no matter the mesoporous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) or electron-blocking \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) scaffold was utilized; this observation indicates that the perovskite itself is able to transfer the electrons across the electrode.[3] Evolving from dye-sensitized solar cells, Snaith and co-workers first demonstrated "meso-superstructured" perovskite solar cells by using a solution process and obtained a PCE of over \(12\%\);[4] a PCE of over \(15\%\) was thereafter achieved by fabricating the vapor-deposited perovskite thin-film solar cells based on a planar heterojunction thin-film architecture.[4,5] Subsequently, several studies based on planar heterojunction perovskite thin-film solar cells have been reported.[6–11] Despite such a rapid progress in the PCE of perovskite solar cells, the role of organic cations played in the light-harvesting process still remains unclear.[12–15] The intrinsic polarization of the metal-halide perovskite, which is a typical characteristic of ferroelectric materials, was proposed as a role influencing the hysteresis.[16–18] Recently, it has been found that one of the main issues for fabricating highly efficient heterojunction thin-film perovskite solar cells lies in the control of the crystallization process during the growth of the perovskite thin films.[19–22] It has been reported that the poor
morphology and crystallinity of the perovskite thin film not only cause electrical shorting but also harm charge transport.[7–9,23] Therefore, some solvent-engineering techniques have been proposed to fabricate the highly uniform perovskite thin films with excellent crystallinity.[24,25] The perovskite thin films with better morphology and crystallinity are much easier to be fabricated by using either the vapor phase deposition or two-step sequential deposition.[5,19–21] However, such approaches involve higher manufacturing cost and longer processing time compared to the one-step and solution-process approach.
Herein, we demonstrate a simple approach to enhance the crystallinity of the obtained perovskite thin film by incorporating metal-organic framework nanocrystals as additives into the precursor solution used for the one-step deposition. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of three-dimensional porous crystalline materials constructed of organic linkers and metal-based nodes.[26,27] Because of their regular nanostructured pores and high surface area,[28] MOFs have been applied for gas storage,[29] separation,[30] chemical sensing,[31] catalysis,[32,33] and electrochromic thin films.[34,35] Besides, the observation of multiferroic behavior in MOFs, particularly in those with perovskite \(\mathrm{ABX}_{3}\) architecture, was extensively studied from both theoretical and experimental sides.[36–41] In our previous study, we have proposed a solvothermal approach to grow the thin films of a Zr-based porphyrin MOF (MOF-525) on conducting glass substrates; the thin film is composed of uniformly grown cubic crystals of MOF-525 with the size ranging from 500 to \(1000\ \mathrm{nm}\).[42] However, to date there are no studies reporting the nanocrystals of MOF-525 smaller than \(500\;\mathrm{nm}\). In this study, the MOF-525 nanocrystals \((\approx\!140\ \mathrm{nm})\) were synthesized. Taking advantages of the smaller crystal size, the MOF-525 nanocrystals can be incorporated with perovskite, and the morphology and crystallinity of the obtained perovskite thin film can be significantly enhanced due to the micropores of the MOF-525 nanocrystals. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report incorporating MOF crystals into perovskite solar cells.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of the activated MOF-525 sample is shown in Figure 1a (pattern (ii)). Diffraction peaks can be observed at \(4.5^{\circ}\), \(6.4^{\circ}\), \(7.8^{\circ}\), and \(9.1^{\circ}\) in the experimental pattern of the MOF-525 sample; all of these peaks agree well with the simulated XRD pattern of MOF-525 reported previously (pattern (i) in Figure 1a).[43] Scanning electron microscopic (SEM) image of the MOF-525 sample is shown in Figure 1b. It can be observed that the sample is composed of several cubic crystals, with the size ranging from 80 to 300 nm; the average size of these cubic crystals is \(140\ \mathrm{nm}\). The
observed cubic morphology is consistent with the morphology of MOF-525 reported previously, but the crystal size obtained here is much smaller compared to that reported in previous studies.[42,43] The nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm of the MOF-525 nanocrystals is shown in Figure 1c. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of the MOF-525 nanocrystals was estimated to be \(2690\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is comparable with the value reported previously \((2620\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})\).[43] Additionally, the pore size distribution of MOF-525 nanocrystals estimated by density functional theory reveals a uniform pore size of \(1.8\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 1d); this pore size is in excellent agreement with the cage size of the MOF-525 crystal structure (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The uniform pores of MOF-525 allow the penetration of the precursor solution of perovskite, which renders the crystallization of perovskite inside the regular micropores. The MOF-525 nanocrystals thus are expected to provide a uniform grain size and an ordered arrangement of perovskite crystallites grown on the conducting substrate.
MOF-525 nanocrystals were incorporated into the precursor solution of perovskite before the spin-coating process for thin-film deposition. Experimental details for the synthesis of MOF-525 nanocrystals, the preparation of the precursor solutions and thin films, and the fabrication of devices are described in the Experimental Section and Supporting Information. To discuss the effect of MOF additive on the performance of perovskite solar cells, three different blending ratios were selected and
named as M0.5, M5, and M50, which mean \(0.5\ \mathrm{v/v}\%\), \(5\ \mathrm{v/v}\%\), and \(50\ \mathrm{v/v}\%\) of the MOF-525 suspension \((20\ \mathrm{mg\ mL^{-1}})\) in the obtained MOF/perovskite precursor solution, respectively (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Two more blending ratios \((2\ \mathrm{v/v}\%\) and \(10\ \mathrm{v/v}\%)\) are discussed in the Supporting Information, as seen in Figure S4 and Table S1 (Supporting Information). The photovoltaic performance of the obtained solar cells using the M0.5, M5, M50, and pristine perovskite thin films is shown in Figure 2a and Table 1. It can be observed that M5 cell shows the best performance among all solar cells, with an average PCE of \(12.0\%\), an open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) of \(0.93\ \mathrm{V}\), a short-circuit current density \((J_{\mathrm{sc}})\) of \(23.08\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}}\), and a fill factor (FF) of \(0.60\); this cell performance is significantly better than that of the control cell fabricated from pristine perovskite precursor solution (average \(\mathrm{PCE}=10.1\%\)). It should be noted that the M5 cell shows significantly improved \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and FF while a comparable \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) compared to the pristine cell. However, the M50 cell shows an obviously lower \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF. The performance of the M0.5 cell seems to be very similar to that of the pristine cell due to the little amount of MOF additive, so this condition will not be included in the following discussions. The current density-voltage \((J-V)\) curve of the best performing M5 solar cell is presented in Figure 2b; a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(0.95\ \mathrm{V}\), a \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(24.03\ \mathrm{mA\ cm^{-2}}\), an FF of \(0.64\), and a PCE of \(14.5\%\) was achieved under AM 1.5G illumination. It should be noted that such a high photocurrent density is attributed to the \(\mathrm{TiCl_{4}}\)-modified \(\mathrm{TiO_{2}}\) compact layer, which has been reported previously.[44] A histogram of the
average PCEs measured from \( 40\,\mathrm{M}5 \) cells which were fabricated separately is shown in the inset of Figure 2b. It can be seen that around \( 60\% \) of M5 cells exhibit the PCEs exceeding \( 12\% \). The incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectrum of the M5 cell (Figure 2c) shows a plateau between 300 and \( 800\;\mathrm{nm} \), which is consistent with the typical characteristic of \( \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Cl}_{x} \) based solar cells.[45] The integrated \( J_{\mathrm{sc}} \) from the IPCE spectrum also agrees with that measured from the \( J{-}V \) curve. The UV–visible spectra of the pristine, M5, and M50 thin films are shown in Figure 2d. Even with a high content of MOF additive in the MOF/perovskite thin film (M50), the obtained UV–visible spectrum is still the same as the pristine one, with a plateau of absorbance covering the full visible range; this feature is consistent with the typical characteristic
of \( \mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Cl}_{x} \).[46] This observation suggests that the spectroscopic property of the meso-tetra(4-carboxyphenyl)porphine (TCPP) linkers containing in the MOF-525 nanocrystals incorporated in the MOF/perovskite thin films would not affect the optical properties of the composite films due to the ultrahigh absorbance of perovskite over the full visible range. Thus, the difference in photovoltaic performance presented in Figure 2a is not attributed to the optical property of the incorporated porphyrin MOF. On the other hand, UV–visible spectrum of the M5 thin film shows a higher absorbance over the full visible range, which may be attributed to either the better morphology or better crystallinity of the perovskite in the composite thin film. Also, a blue shift can be clearly observed in the UV–visible spectra of MOF/perovskite thin films compared to that of the pristine perovskite thin film. This observation indicates that the MOF/perovskite thin films perform more like a “meso-scaffold” configuration instead of a planar one.[47]
Since a significant difference in the crystallization process of perovskite was observed during the heating process after spin coating the pristine, M5, and M50 precursor solutions (see Figure S5 and its corresponding discussion in the Supporting Information), we speculated that the better photovoltaic performance of the M5 cell may be attributed to the better morphology or crystallinity of the obtained perovskite caused by the MOF additive. The top-view SEM images of the pristine, M5, and M50 thin films at different magnifications are presented in Figure 3. The pristine thin film shows a poor coverage over the underlying layer, as evidenced by several exposed regions of
\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) compact layer surface. It can be seen that the M5 thin film reveals a much better surface coverage with a few MOF-525 nanocrystals embed into the perovskite layer; this better morphology may be the reason for the higher \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) and FF of the M5 solar cell compared to the pristine one. On the contrary, for the M50 thin film, numerous MOF-525 nanocrystals cover on the perovskite layer nonuniformly. The overdose of MOF additive results in the phase separation between the MOF-525 nanocrystals and perovskite; this observation agrees with the macroscopic morphology observed in the photograph (Figure S5b, Supporting Information). The M50 cell which gives low \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) and FF is due to the insulating nature of MOF-525. The cross-section SEM images of these thin films also support such observations (Figure S6, Supporting Information).
To investigate the difference in crystallinity of the perovskite and MOF/perovskite thin films, XRD was utilized (Figure 4a). The strong diffraction peaks at \(14.8^{\circ}\) and \(29.0^{\circ}\) can be correctly indexed as (110) and (220) planes of the perovskite crystal structure.[5] It can be observed that the intensities of (110) peaks are nearly the same in all the three patterns. However, a noticeable increase in the intensity of (220) peak can be observed in the XRD patterns of both M5 and M50 thin films; this result indicates that the presence of MOF-525 nanocrystals enhances the crystallinity of the obtained perovskite in the composite thin films.
Photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the pristine, M5, and M50 thin films, measured at the excitation of \(600\,\mathrm{nm}\), are shown in Figure 4b. Interestingly, an increasing blue shift of the PL peak can be observed when the amount of MOF additive increases. This observation suggests that with a higher blending ratio of MOF, the MOF/perovskite thin film performs closer to a "meso-scaffold" configuration reported previously;[47] the MOF-525 nanocrystals may act as a microporous scaffold to allow the crystallization of perovskite to occur inside. Moreover, the full width at half maximum of the PL peak increases when moving from the pristine to M50, indicating that the amount of impurity increases when moving from the pristine to M50 thin film. This result confirms that the incorporation of MOF-525 nanocrystals is successful in both M5 and M50 thin films.
Since the UV–visible spectra of the MOF/perovskite thin films (Figure 2d) do not reveal any well-defined characteristic peak of MOF-525, the pristine, M5, and M50 thin films were digested in NaOH solutions for UV–visible measurements (Figure 4c). For the digested pristine perovskite thin film, no absorption peaks can be observed. The UV–visible spectrum of the digested M0.5 thin film was also measured, which gives the same spectrum as the pristine one; this result confirms that no MOF-525 nanocrystals are incorporated in the M0.5 thin film. However, both the digested M5 and M50 samples clearly show a Soret band at \(469\,\mathrm{nm}\) and three Q bands located between 550 and \(700\,\mathrm{nm}\). This result further confirms that the MOF-525 nanocrystals are incorporated in both M5 and M50 thin films. In addition, this spectrum is consistent with the reported UV–visible spectrum of the TCPP metalated with \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) (PbTCPP),[48] indicating that the TCPP linkers of MOF-525 nanocrystals in both M5 and M50 thin films were postmetallated during the crystallization process of perovskite. This finding confirms that the \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3-x}\mathrm{Cl}_{x}\) precursor solution can diffuse into the micropores of MOF-525 nanocrystals, and further suggests that the crystallization of perovskite taking place inside the micropores during the heating process; this phenomenon is reasonable since the size of perovskite unit cell is much smaller than the pore size of MOF-525.[49]
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of both M5 and M50 thin films were measured after various periods of \(\mathrm{Ar}^{+}\)etching in order to investigate the elemental content of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) at different depths of the films away from the top surface (see Figure S7 and its corresponding discussion in the Supporting Information); the atomic ratios (\%) of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) of both films measured after various etching periods are shown in Figure 4d. A significantly high content of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) can be observed on the M50 thin film at short etching periods, which is caused by numerous MOF-525 nanocrystals covered on the top of the M50 thin film (Figure 3). Except this observation, it should be noted that the atomic ratio of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) increases with the increasing etching period for both the M5 and M50 thin films. This result suggests that the MOF-525 nanocrystals tend to be incorporated near the
bottom of the MOF/perovskite thin film. Since the crystallization of perovskite started from the bottom during the heating process, the ordered microporous scaffold would provide a regular arrangement of perovskite crystallites during the initial stage of crystallization. Thus, a better crystallinity of perovskite can be achieved even with a small amount of MOF additive (M5). However, the MOF/perovskite solar cells fabricated in this study still show poor moisture stability due to the fact that Spiro-OMeTAD is utilized as the hole transport material; this material possesses poor mechanical toughness so that it cannot prevent the MOF/perovskite thin films from moisture degradation, which has been widely recognized.[50–53] Other MOFs with different pore sizes, which are not constructed by porphyrin-based linkers, were also synthesized according to the reported procedures [54] and incorporated into the perovskite solar cells (see Figures S8 and S9, and corresponding discussion in the Supporting Information). The result further confirms that the porphyrin-based linkers in MOF-525 have nothing to do with photovoltaic performances. Besides, due to the insulated nature of these three MOFs, we believe that the perovskite thin films still remain direct band gap property after adding nanocrystals of MOFs as an additive.[55,56] Instead, the pore structure of MOFs incorporated in perovskite solar cells plays an important role in the obtained cell performance; this observation implies that incorporating other MOFs with appropriate pore sizes and pore shapes may further enhance the performance of perovskite solar cells.
In summary, a significant enhancement in the morphology and crystallinity of the perovskite thin film was achieved via adding microporous nanocrystals of MOF-525 as an additive. The microporous MOF-525 nanocrystals incorporated near the bottom of the MOF/perovskite composite thin film act as a regular scaffold to allow the crystallization of perovskite to happen inside; such a regular scaffold thus provides an ordered arrangement of perovskite crystallites during the initial stage of crystallization. Ongoing work is focused on incorporating other microporous or mesoporous MOF materials with different pore shapes and sizes inside the perovskite thin film.
## Experimental Section
Synthesis of MOF-525 Nanocrystals. The MOF-525 nanocrystals were synthesized by the procedure modified from the one reported in our previous study.[42] Briefly, the MOF-525 nanocrystals were synthesized by a solvothermal process operated in an oven at \(80\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) using benzoic acid (1.35 g), zirconyl chloride octahydrate (105 mg), and mesotetra(4-carboxyphenyl)porphine \(\left(\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{T C P P},\ 47\ \mathsf{\ m g}\right)\) in DMF \((8\ \mathsf{m L})\). The experimental details for the synthesis and activation of the MOF-525 nanocrystals and the preparation of the suspension of MOF-525 nanocrystals were described in the Supporting Information.
Preparation of the MOF/Perovskite Precursors: The experimental details for the synthesis of methylammonium iodide (MAI) were described in the Supporting Information. The precursor solution of perovskite was prepared by dissolving MAI (2.64 m) and lead chloride (99.999%, Sigma–Aldrich, 0.88 M) in anhydrous DMF (99.8%, Sigma–Aldrich).
The MOF/perovskite precursor solutions were prepared via mixing the MOF-525 suspension \((20\,\mathrm{\mg/mL})\) and the precursor solution of perovskite with different volume ratios. The solutions were stirred at room temperature for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) and filtered with \(0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) PVDF filters before spin coating.
Fabrication of Thin-Film Perovskite Solar Cells: Fluorine-doped tin oxide (TEC-7, NSG America, Inc., 7 \(\Omega\ \square^{-1}\)) glass substrates were ultrasonically cleaned sequentially in a neutral cleaner solution, deionized water, acetone, and isopropyl alcohol for \(15~\mathsf{m i n}\) . After drying under \(\mathsf{N}_{2},\) the substrates were further cleaned by ozone treatment for 15 min. To deposit a dense \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) compact layer, titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) solution (Sigma–Aldrich, \(75\ \ \mathsf{w t}\%\) in isopropyl alcohol, \(20\times10^{-3}\) M) was spin coated onto the substrate at \(3000~{\mathsf{r p m}}\) for \(30~\mathsf{s}\) and heated at \(125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5~\mathsf{m i n}\) ; this depositing procedure was conducted for three times successively. The obtained substrate was then sintered at \(550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15~\mathsf{m i n}\) . Thereafter, the obtained substrate was immersed in aqueous solution of \(\mathsf{T i C l}_{4}\) (Sigma–Aldrich, \(0.02~\mathsf{M})\) at \(70~^{\circ}\subset\) for \(60\,\mathsf{\,m i n}\) , and washed with deionized water. The obtained substrate was further sintered at \(550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(15~\mathsf{m i n}\) . Then the perovskite or MOF/perovskite precursor solution was spin coated onto the obtained substrate at 2000 rpm for \(30~\mathsf{s}\) . After the spin coating, the substrate was immediately transferred to a hotplate at \(100~^{\circ}\subset\) and heated for \(50~\mathsf{m i n}\) . The perovskite or MOF/perovskite thin film was thus obtained.
A solution of spiro-MeOTAD (Merck, \(96~\mathrm{~mg}\) ) in chlorobenzene (Sigma–Aldrich) was mixed with 4-tertbutylpyridine (tBP, \(10~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) and bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt (LiTFSI, \(170\;\;\mathsf{m g\;\;m L^{-1}}\) , \(40~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) solution in acetonitrile, and then the obtained solution was spin coated on the perovskite or MOF/perovskite thin film at 1500 rpm for \(45\ s\) . Before depositing the silver electrode, spiro-MeOTAD was allowed to be oxidized in air over night at room temperature. Finally, Ag electrode was deposited by thermal evaporation. The active area of the electrode was fixed at \(0.10\;\mathsf{c m}^{2}\) , and all experiments were operated in air with \(30\%\) relative humidity in a dry room. During the photovoltaic measurements, all solar cells were masked with a thin mask, which was used to define the active area to be \(0.10\;\mathsf{c m}^{2}\) .
Further experimental details regarding the instrumentation for synthesis and material characterizations were included in the Supporting Information.
